20:03:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: At the geographic poles, the concepts of east and west become ambiguous because all lines of longitude converge. Here’s how to differentiate them: - **At the North Pole**: Every direction you face is south. East and west are defined by the direction of rotation of the Earth. If you stand at the North Pole, east is the direction of the Earth’s rotation (counterclockwise when viewed from above the North Pole). In practice, you can define east as the direction in which the sun rises (though at the pole, the sun may be up for six months and then down for six months). Alternatively, you can use a compass: a magnetic compass points toward magnetic north, but true north is the geographic pole. To find east, face south (away from the pole), and east is to your left (if you are in the Northern Hemisphere). But at the pole itself, "left" depends on your orientation. - **At the South Pole**: Every direction is north. East is still defined by Earth’s rotation (clockwise when viewed from above the South Pole). If you face away from the pole (north), east is to your right. Again, the sun’s apparent motion (if visible) can help: at the South Pole, the sun circles the sky clockwise, so east is the direction from which it appears to move. In summary, at the poles, east and west are not fixed geographical directions but are relative to your orientation and the Earth’s rotation. The most practical method is to use a reference line (like a meridian) or a compass corrected for magnetic declination, but even then, the concept is largely theoretical because you are at a point where all longitudes meet.
